episode, we started

00:00:02.093 --> 00:00:03.510
looking at best
practices that you

00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:06.630
can use to get the most
value out of Cloud Logging.

00:00:06.630 --> 00:00:08.490
We covered how to
ingest and store logs,

00:00:08.490 --> 00:00:11.040
how to centralize them, and
how to manage log access

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:12.000
and retention.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:14.700
Today, we'll wrap up
this topic by looking

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:16.920
at how you can use advanced
log queries to find

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:18.690
the exact logs
you're looking for

00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:20.700
and how to manage logging costs.

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:23.640
Welcome to "Engineering for
Reliability with Google Cloud."

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:31.400
Let's start with using logs for
troubleshooting and debugging.

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:34.070
I'm going to show you how to
use advanced queries to quickly

00:00:34.070 --> 00:00:35.510
find the logs
you're looking for,

00:00:35.510 --> 00:00:38.210
and how to use error
reporting to automatically

00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:42.420
identify, classify, and
track application crashes.

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:45.480
An advanced log query is
the Boolean expression

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:47.700
that specifies a
subset of all the log

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:49.420
entries in your project.

00:00:49.420 --> 00:00:51.930
You can use them to choose
log entries from specific logs

00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:54.540
or services,
specify time ranges,

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:57.390
match specific metadata
or user-defined fields,

00:00:57.390 --> 00:00:58.920
and even sample logs.

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:01.230
Let's have a look.

00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:04.066
From the Project Dashboard,
let's go to logging and select

00:01:04.066 --> 00:01:04.649
Logs Explorer.

00:01:07.750 --> 00:01:09.280
In previous
episodes, you've seen

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:12.290
how to use the log's field
explorer to, for example,

00:01:12.290 --> 00:01:15.490
select air logs being emitted
by our front end service running

00:01:15.490 --> 00:01:16.270
on a GKU cluster.

00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:22.850
Notice that our
selections are actually

00:01:22.850 --> 00:01:24.680
converted into a query.

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:29.260
We can modify the query directly
without using field selections.

00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:31.980
For example, we can negate
one of our expressions

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:34.035
to look for all logs
that aren't errors.

00:01:36.730 --> 00:01:40.330
If we want to only get the logs
for a particular time interval,

00:01:40.330 --> 00:01:43.390
we can easily specify that
using the Time Stamp field.

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:54.180
We can even retrieve
just a sample of the logs

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:55.575
by using the Sample function.

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:05.790
If this is a query
we'll need later,

00:02:05.790 --> 00:02:08.130
we can save it,
and then access it

00:02:08.130 --> 00:02:10.479
whenever we need it in the
future from the Saved Queries

00:02:10.479 --> 00:02:10.979
tab.

00:02:19.570 --> 00:02:22.600
So you can use advanced queries
to quickly and easily get

00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:23.710
the logs that you want.

00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:29.240
Saving your queries allows you
to get back to a set of logs

00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:32.910
you need to review
on a regular basis.

00:02:32.910 --> 00:02:34.650
Advanced log queries
are a great way

00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:37.260
to quickly find the exact
logs you're looking for.

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:39.862
But what if this
was even easier?

00:02:39.862 --> 00:02:42.070
You may remember that we
covered error reporting back

00:02:42.070 --> 00:02:43.012
in episode 9.

00:02:43.012 --> 00:02:44.470
If you haven't seen
it, no worries.

00:02:44.470 --> 00:02:46.540
We'll link to it in the
episode notes below.

00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:49.630
Just remember that it's an easy
way to track, analyze, and get

00:02:49.630 --> 00:02:53.060
alerted on errors
in your application.

00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:54.930
Advanced log queries
and error reporting

00:02:54.930 --> 00:02:56.930
are really useful if
you're trying to understand

00:02:56.930 --> 00:02:58.790
the behavior of your services.

00:02:58.790 --> 00:03:01.160
Being able to quickly find
the logs you're looking for

00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:03.470
and easily track
application crashes

00:03:03.470 --> 00:03:06.350
should definitely help you
keep your services healthy.

00:03:06.350 --> 00:03:09.530
Finally, let's get into
managing logging costs

00:03:09.530 --> 00:03:12.560
which is a key part of
optimizing your Cloud spend.

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:14.810
I want to show you how to
view your logging usage,

00:03:14.810 --> 00:03:17.580
how to track your
logging costs over time,

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:20.750
and how to control the logs
that you ingest and store.

00:03:20.750 --> 00:03:23.570
First, let's have a look at how
to use information in the Cloud

00:03:23.570 --> 00:03:26.990
Console to understand how much
of your bill is due to logging

00:03:26.990 --> 00:03:29.330
and how much logging is
being ingested and stored

00:03:29.330 --> 00:03:31.540
in your project.

00:03:31.540 --> 00:03:33.655
From the Billing home
page, let's go to Reports.

00:03:37.340 --> 00:03:40.280
Here, we can select our project
and the Cloud Logging service.

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:50.700
This shows us the
logging charges

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:52.207
we've incurred for
the current month

00:03:52.207 --> 00:03:53.415
or for another time interval.

00:03:56.240 --> 00:03:59.370
If we want to understand where
these costs are coming from,

00:03:59.370 --> 00:04:05.560
we can go to logging log
storage and look at the usage

00:04:05.560 --> 00:04:09.530
details for our default bucket.

00:04:09.530 --> 00:04:11.630
This takes us to
Metrics Explorer

00:04:11.630 --> 00:04:14.060
and shows us the
volume of logs ingested

00:04:14.060 --> 00:04:17.827
by the various resources
in our project.

00:04:17.827 --> 00:04:19.660
So that's how you can
use the billing report

00:04:19.660 --> 00:04:22.450
to stay on top of your logging
costs and the utilization

00:04:22.450 --> 00:04:27.670
metrics to track where the
logs are actually coming from.

00:04:27.670 --> 00:04:29.410
Understanding your
logging costs is

00:04:29.410 --> 00:04:31.370
key to being able
to manage them.

00:04:31.370 --> 00:04:33.220
But actually
controlling those costs,

00:04:33.220 --> 00:04:35.380
is primarily accomplished
by reducing the amount

00:04:35.380 --> 00:04:37.930
of logs you ingest and store.

00:04:37.930 --> 00:04:40.750
We covered log retention
in our last episode.

00:04:40.750 --> 00:04:42.340
Remember that you
are not charged

00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:45.040
for storing logs for the
default retention period.

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:47.980
As such, you can reduce
your log storage costs

00:04:47.980 --> 00:04:49.990
by carefully
considering whether you

00:04:49.990 --> 00:04:52.780
need to retain logs in
Cloud Logging for longer

00:04:52.780 --> 00:04:54.280
than a default retention.

00:04:54.280 --> 00:04:56.860
If you do need those
logs or other use cases,

00:04:56.860 --> 00:04:59.110
consider exporting
them to Google Cloud

00:04:59.110 --> 00:05:02.680
Storage for archival or
BigQuery for analytics.

00:05:02.680 --> 00:05:05.440
Your primary mechanism
to reduce ingestion costs

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:07.150
are log exclusions.

00:05:07.150 --> 00:05:10.420
Exclusions are configured
when you set up log sinks.

00:05:10.420 --> 00:05:13.120
If you're looking to reduce the
volume of logs being ingested,

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:16.970
you can modify the default sink
to add an exclusion filter.

00:05:16.970 --> 00:05:18.520
Remember that
excluded log entries

00:05:18.520 --> 00:05:22.120
won't be available in the Logs
Explorer or Cloud Debugger.

00:05:22.120 --> 00:05:24.430
Log entries that aren't
routed to at least one

00:05:24.430 --> 00:05:28.000
log bucket are also excluded
from error reporting.

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:30.730
But with user-defined,
logs-based metrics,

00:05:30.730 --> 00:05:34.120
our computers can log entries
in both included and excluded

00:05:34.120 --> 00:05:35.540
logs.

00:05:35.540 --> 00:05:38.140
Of course, the other way to
reduce log ingestion costs

00:05:38.140 --> 00:05:41.060
is to produce less logs
from your applications.

00:05:41.060 --> 00:05:43.870
For example, you can carefully
adjust the log levels

00:05:43.870 --> 00:05:45.820
to control log volume.

00:05:45.820 --> 00:05:48.400
This is especially useful
for serverless services,

00:05:48.400 --> 00:05:51.340
where there's no way
to view excluded logs.

00:05:51.340 --> 00:05:54.160
Consider both managing log
volume in your application

00:05:54.160 --> 00:05:59.370
and using exclusions to balance
observability and costs.

00:05:59.370 --> 00:06:00.880
Thanks for joining me today.

00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:03.780
We covered best practices for
querying logs in Cloud Logging

00:06:03.780 --> 00:06:05.820
and managing your logging costs.

00:06:05.820 --> 00:06:07.230
I hope these
recommendations help

00:06:07.230 --> 00:06:10.330
you keep your service
reliable and your users happy.

00:06:10.330 --> 00:06:11.770
Thanks so much for
watching today.

